## Discussion
In this study, we identify two ubiquitin-specific proteases that are physically and functionally associated with human PRC1 complexes and provide novel insights into their modes of action. At first sight, it seemed odd to find two distinct USPs associated with a complex whose principal function is to act as an E3 ubiquitin ligase for histone H2A (Cao et al, 2005; Buchwald et al, 2006; Li et al, 2006; Elderkin et al, 2007). USP7 and USP11 show no obvious similarities in their primary sequence apart from the catalytic domains common to all USPs (Nijman et al, 2005), and although they have been classified within the same group of USPs based on the number and functional diversity of their binding partners (Sowa et al, 2009), there are few overlaps among the interacting proteins.
Curiously, PcG proteins were not identified in these screens, although there have been previous reports that USP7 copurifies with PRC1 components in both human and insect cells (van der Knaap et al, 2005; Sanchez et al, 2007). Our
The physiological relevance of this interaction remains unclear as USP7 and USP11 can function independently and have different target specificities. Although USP11 has been shown to interact with a diverse array of cellular proteins (Bouwmeester et al, 2004; Yamaguchi et al, 2007), its deubiquitination activity has only been shown for a few substrates (Ideguchi et al, 2002; Lin et al, 2008). Here, we show that USP11 is capable of removing ubiquitin from several components of the PRC1 complex, including BMI1, MEL18 and RING1, but had no apparent effect on the ubiquitination status of H2A and H2B under the assay conditions used.
In contrast, recombinant USP7 was able to de-ubiquitinate both H2A and H2B in vitro. Given the precedents in Drosophila (van der Knaap et al, 2005; Sanchez et al, 2007), we initially thought that human USP7 might be contributing to PcG-mediated silencing by reversing the mono-ubiquitination of histone H2B. However, in the in vitro assay, we did not observe specificity for H2Bub over H2Aub. As the purified USP7 used in these experiments was extracted under high salt conditions, it is possible that an essential co-factor, such as GMP synthetase (van der Knaap et al, 2005), was missing. To try to circumvent the possible limitations of in vitro assays, we also asked whether knockdown of USP7 in FDFs would change the global levels of H2Aub and H2Bub. However, we saw no discernible increase in the extent of ubiquitination on these histones (data not shown). Conversely, over-expression of USP7 did not result in a significant decrease in H2Aub and H2Bub levels. As the human genome encodes at least four times as many deubiquitinating enzymes as Drosophila, it is possible that a different USP has assumed the specialised function of regulating the ubiquitination of H2B in human cells.
It is conceivable that mono- and poly-ubiquitination have different effects in this setting, but the simplest explanation of our findings would be that USP7 and USP11 control the ubiquitin-mediated turnover of the PRC1 proteins. Importantly, knockdown of either USP7 or USP11 reduces the total and chromatin-bound pool of BMI1 and MEL18, and the effects can be mitigated by inhibiting proteasome function. From a functional standpoint, this reduces the availability of PRC1 components resulting in de-repression of target genes such as INK4a. Thus, knockdown of either USP7 or USP11 in primary human fibroblasts resulted in loss of PRC1 and PRC2 occupancy at the INK4a promoter and up-regulation of \(\mathrm{p}16^{\mathrm{INK4a}}\). The effects were consistently observed with independent shRNAs, and the obvious phenotypic consequence was a senescence-like proliferative arrest.
The initial motivation for this study was to better understand the transcriptional regulation of the INK4a tumour suppressor, and in this regard, we have added two ubiquitin-specific proteases, USP7 and USP11, to the extensive list of proteins that are directly or indirectly involved. A future priority will be to explore the physiological contexts in which USP7 and USP11 might be deployed to regulate PRC1 function. The central function of ubiquitination in the regulation of INK4a and the possibility that USPs can promote the bypass of oncogene-induced senescence by stabilising the PRC1 complex provide opportunities for using specific USP inhibitors in a variety of therapeutic contexts.
## Materials and methods
## Tissue culture
Human dermal fibroblasts (FDF and Leiden strains), 293T- and 293T-derived cell lines were cultured in Dulbecco modified Eagle's
medium supplemented with \(10\%\) foetal calf serum, \(100\,\mathrm{IU/ml}\) penicillin and \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}\) streptomycin. Lentiviral vector production and infections were performed as described (Maertens et al, 2009). Stably expressing cell lines were made by infecting 293T cells with VSV-G—pseudotyped retrovirus carrying the pQMEL18-Flag-puro, pQBM11-Flag-puro, pQFlag-USP7-puro, pQFlag-USP11-puro, or pQFlag-H2A-puro vectors. The cells were selected with \(2.5\,\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}\) puromycin. In some experiments, cells were treated with \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) MG132 for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) to inhibit proteasome function. The control cells were treated with DMSO. Cell proliferation assays were performed by staining with crystal violet, as previously described (McConnell et al, 1998). Five days post-selection, \(5\times10^{3}\) cells were plated out into each well of a 24-well plate and 6 wells were used per time point.
## Plasmid construction
MEL18 ORF was amplified from pGM-MEL18-cTAP (Elderkin et al, 2007) using primers GM153 and GM169. The PCR product was digested with BamHI and MfI and ligated with BamHI- and EcoRI-digested pGM-Ram2-Flag, giving pGM-MEL18-Flag. To create pQMEL18-Flag-puro, the insert generated from HindIII and XbaI digestion of pGM-MEL18-Flag was treated with Pfu Ultra in the presence of dNTPs to fill in the overhangs, and ligated into filled in NotI-EcoRI-cut pQHA puro. PGM-BM1-Flag was generated by amplifying the BM11 ORF from pGBT (Elderkin et al, 2007) using primers GM230 and GM238. The PCR fragment digested with MfI and BamHI was ligated with BamHI- and EcoRI-digested pGM-Ram2-Flag (Maertens et al, 2006). For the generation of pQBM11-Flag puro, pGM-BM11-Flag was digested with BamHI and XhoI and ligated with BamHI- and XhoI-cut pQMEL18-Flag-puro. The constructs expressing CBX2-Flag/HA, CBX4-Flag/HA, CBX6-Flag/HA, CBX7-Flag/HA and CBX8-Flag/HA are described elsewhere (El Messaoudi et al, 2000). Flag-histone H2A was expressed from pQFlag-H2A-puro, which was generated by amplifying H2A with primers GM191 and GM192 using cDNA made from 293T cells. The amplicon was digested with Agel and BamHI and ligated into Agel- and BamHI-cut pQFlag-puro. To purify CBX8-Flag from bacteria, the CBX8-Flag ORF was amplified from pGM-CBX8-Flag (Maertens et al, 2009) with primers GM300 and GM301. The amplicon was digested with BglII and ligated into SmaI- and BamHI-cut pCPH6P-BIV IN (Checpanov, 2007), replacing the BIV IN sequence, giving pGMH6P-CBX8-Flag. The constructs used for baculovirus expression of Bmi1, Mel18 and Ring1b were described elsewhere (Elderkin et al, 2007). All constructs were sequenced verified. Lentiviral vectors targeting the USP7 or USP11 transcript were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich; USP7sh1: NM_003470 1249; USP7sh2: NM_003470 1648; USP11sh1: NM_0046512 1800; USP11sh2: NM_0046512 1695.
## Immunoblotting
Cells were harvested by trypsinisation and washed extensively in ice-cold phosphate-buffered saline (PBS). Proteins were extracted in five volumes of RIPAS (high salt containing RIPA buffer: \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\) Hepes-NaOH, pH 7.6, \(0.3\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl, \(0.01\,\%\) NP-40, \(1\,\%\) sodium deoxycholate, \(0.1\,\%\) SDS and \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA in the presence of protease inhibitors (Roche) and \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) PMSF). Protein concentration was determined using BCA assay (Pierce) and samples \((10{-}40\,\upmu\mathrm{g})\) of total protein were separated on a \(12\,\%\) SDS–PAGE gel. Proteins were transferred onto nitrocellulose membrane and immunoblotted using the following dilutions of antibody: rabbit anti-MEL18:1:500 (Santa Cruz), goat anti-MEL18 1:1000 (Abcam), mouse anti-Flag M2 1:3000 (Sigma-Aldrich), mouse anti-Flag conjugated to horse radish peroxidase (HRP) 1:3000 (Sigma-Aldrich), rabbit anti-RING1 (Cell Signaling), mouse anti-BMI1 (1:500) (Abcam), rabbit anti- \(\cdot\upbeta\) -tubulin 1:1000 (Santa Cruz), rabbit anti-TFID1 (TRP) 1:1000 (Santa Cruz), mouse anti-HA 16B12 1:2000 (Covance), rabbit anti-HA 1:1000 (Santa Cruz), mouse anti-p16 JC81:5, mouse anti-p53 1:1000 (DO-1, Santa Cruz), mouse anti-cyclin D1 1:1000 (BD Pharmingen), mouse anti-GAPDH HRP-conjugated 1:10 000 (Abcam), rabbit anti-USP7 1:520 (Abcam), rabbit anti-CBX6 (Bethyl Laboratories), rabbit anti-CBX7 (Abcam), rabbit anti-histone H2A (Upstate) and rabbit anti-histone H2B (Upstate). For the generation of polyclonal anti-USP11 antiserum, three synthetic peptides corresponding to amino acids 587–605, 716–734 and 909–920 were fused to Keyhole Limpet Hemocyanin, and a mixture of the three peptides was used to immunise rabbits. The IgG fraction was purified from the isolated serum and used at a 1:500 dilution. HRP-conjugated secondary antibodies were purchased from GE Healthcare and used at 1:2000 dilution. The blots were developed using ECL plus (Pierce). In some experiments, recombinant proteins were detected with rabbit anti-Flag antibody 1:1000 (Cell Signaling) followed by 1:2000 dilution of alkaline phosphatase-conjugated anti-rabbit IgG (Sigma). The blots were stained by washing in \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 9.5 followed by incubation in staining solution ( \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 9.5, \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) , \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(0.33\,\mathrm{mg/ml}\) nitro blue tetrazolium chloride (Sigma) and \(0.165\,\mathrm{mg/ml}\) 5-bromo-4-chloro-3 indolyl phosphate (Sigma)). The staining was stopped by rinsing the blots with water followed by air drying.
Laemmli buffer. Samples \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{l})\) of the relevant fractions were separated on \(10\,\%\) SDS–PAGE gels and the proteins were detected by immunoblotting.
## Purification of recombinant proteins and glutathione-S-transferase pull downs
To produce GST, GST-USP7 and GST-USP11, the Rosetta (DE3) strain (Novagen) was transformed with the corresponding recombinant-expression vectors. Expression was induced with \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) IPTG at \(18\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}\) overnight. The cells were harvested and lysed in ice-cold sonication buffer \((50\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA, \(0.25\,\%\) Triton-X-100, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) PMSF) supplemented with lysozyme. All further procedures were performed at \(4\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}\). The sonicated sample was cleared by centrifugation at \(16\,000\,\mathrm{g}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) and the supernatant was run through a column of \(1\,\mathrm{ml}\) pre-equilibrated glutathione sepharose resin (GE Healthcare). The column was washed extensively in \(50\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA, \(500\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) PMSF and the proteins were eluted in \(50\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 8.0, \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\) glutathione. Positive fractions were pooled, dialysed overnight in \(25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, \(150\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) DTT, \(10\,\%\) glycerol and concentrated on Microcon spin columns with a MW cutoff of \(10\,\mathrm{kDa}\) (Millipore). Glycerol was added to final concentration of \(20\,\%\) , and the proteins were snap frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at \(-80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Full-length mouse Mel18, Bmi1 and Ring1B were fused to a \(\mathrm{His}_{12}\mathrm{FLAG}\) tag and cloned into pDEST8 using the Gateway cloning system (Invitrogen). Recombinant baculovirus was generated using the Bac-to-Bac baculovirus system (Invitrogen). Wild-type virus, vMel18-FlagHis, vBmi1-FlagHis and vRing1B-FlagHis were used to infect Sf9 insect cells for \(60\,\mathrm{h}\). Cells were harvested by centrifugation at \(1500\,\mathrm{g}\) and resuspended in lysis buffer \((20\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.5, \(250\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(10\,\%\) glycerol, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA, \(0.1\,\%\) triton, \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) 2-mecaptoethanol and protease inhibitors). The lysate was then sonicated for five \(30\,\mathrm{s}\) cycles, followed by centrifugation at \(15\,000\,\mathrm{r.p.m}\) . to \(2\,\mathrm{h}\). The cell lysate was loaded onto a \(5\,\mathrm{ml}\) Hi-Trap \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) chelating column (GE Healthcare) equilibrated with Buffer A \((20\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.5, \(500\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl and \(10\,\%\) glycerol) and washed with 10 column volumes of Buffer A containing \(40\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole. Proteins were eluted with a gradient of \(40{-}1000\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole in buffer A over \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) , volumes. Protein samples were then dialysed against storage buffer \((25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.5, \(10\,\%\) glycerol, \(150\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) DTT) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) with two changes. Aliquots were frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at \(-80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
To produce CBX8-Flag, the Rosetta2 (DE3) strain (Novagen) was transformed with pGMHP6-CBX8-Flag. \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\)-CBX8-Flag expression was induced during \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) using \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) IPTG. The cells were harvested and lysed in ice-cold core buffer \((25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl) supplemented with \(0.5\,\%\) Triton-X-100, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) PMSF and lysozyme. All procedures were performed at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The sonicated sample was cleared by centrifugation at \(16\,000\,\mathrm{g}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The supernatant was supplemented with \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole and run over a column prepared with \(3\,\mathrm{ml}\) NiNA resin (Qiagen). After extensive washing, the protein was eluted with core buffer supplemented with \(200\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole. Positive fractions were pooled and the \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\)-tag was removed by Percission protease cleavage overnight at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the presence of \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) DTT. The cleaved protein was diluted five-fold in ice-cold \(25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, to reduce the NaCl concentration to \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) and run over a cation exchange column (GE Healthcare). After extensive washes, the protein was eluted using a linear gradient of \(0.1{-}1\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl in \(25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4 (CBX8-Flag eluted at \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl). The positive fractions were pooled and further purified by gel filtration in a HiLoad 16/60 Superdex-200 column (GE Healthcare) in \(25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 7.4, \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl. Purified CBX8-Flag was concentrated to \(2.5\,\mathrm{mg/ml}\), supplemented with \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) DTT, \(10\,\%\) glycerol, snap frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at \(-80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
The Flag-tagged full-length MEL18, BM11, RING1, RING2 and CBX8 proteins were expressed using the TNT T7-coupled reticulocyte lysate system (Promega), according to the manufacturer's instructions. The templates were prepared by linearisation of pGM-MEL18-Flag, pGM-BMI1-Flag, pGM-Flag-RING1, pGM-FlagRING2 and pGM-Flag-CBX8 with EcoRV. Each \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) in vitro
transcription/translation reaction contained \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\) linearised plasmid, \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{Ci}\) of \(\left[^{35}\mathrm{S}\right]\)-methionine (GE Healthcare) and \(25\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) reticulocyte lysate. The GST pull-down assays were performed as described in Maertens et al (2006) using \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of in vitro translated product per reaction.
## Ubiquitination assays
To enrich for ubiquitinated proteins, 293T- or 293T-derived cells were transfected with pMT107 (Treier et al, 1994) to express \(\mathrm{His}_{6}\)-ubiquitin. After \(36\,\mathrm{h}\), the cells were washed and harvested by scraping in ice-cold PBS. After centrifugation, the cells were resuspended in \(0.5\,\mathrm{ml}\) PBS and an input sample \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{l})\) was mixed with \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\,2\times\) Laemmli sample buffer and boiled for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\). The remaining cells were resuspended in \(1\,\mathrm{ml}\) buffer A (6 M guanidinium hydrochloride, \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HPO}_{4}/\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) pH 8.0, \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole) and sonicated. The extract was incubated with \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) NiNT5 agarose (Qiagen) for \(5\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. The beads were washed twice in buffer A, twice in buffer A/TI (3 volumes buffer TI and 1 volume buffer A) and once in buffer T1 \((25\,\mathrm{mM}\) Tris–HCl pH 6.8, \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole). The ubiquitinated proteins were eluted by boiling for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) in Laemmli sample buffer supplemented with \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA, separated in \(10\,\%\) SDS–PAGE gels and detected by immunoblotting.
